The inclusion-ordered set $\mathcal C(T)$ is a complete lattice with largest element $\top_T$. Write $\bot_T$ for its least element. For an actual product $T=\Pi x:D.E$, choose a set $A\subseteq\top_D$. For each $n\in A$ choose any family of candidates $C_{n,a}\in\mathcal C(T_n)$ indexed by a set $Q_n$. Then $$\{h\in\top_T:\text{for every }n\in A\text{ and }a\in Q_n,
                                  \ h n\in C_{n,a}\}
\label{eq:product-test-candidate}$$ is a candidate at $T$.

Under $h:\operatorname{Resp}(v)$, let $P_x=\operatorname{Pair}(\operatorname{Part}(v),x^0)$. For every $x':X$ there are proofs $$\begin{aligned}
 P_x@(x'^{\,\ell})&\leftrightarrow
                         v\diamond_m(x'^{\,0}), \label{rel-pair-left}\\
 P_x@(x'^{\,r})&\leftrightarrow x\simeq x'. \label{rel-pair-right}
\end{aligned}$$
